{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfbHDFwNsv9f73YyVA27G","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I rewrote the repair candidate as **`side_bias_streak_aversion_lapse`**, and it passes the self-check. That check doesn't test novelty, though, so the near-duplicate rejection could still happen at admission.\n\n**What changed.** The first attempt added an indifference band to `individual_streak_aversion_lapse`. Its predictions came out almost the same as that model's (RMSE 0.00195, just under the 0.002 threshold). The likely reason is that the band shrank to near zero during fitting, though I didn't check its fitted value. This version swaps the band for a different decision-rule mechanism, which the brief allows:\n\n- **Evidence:** the same as `individual_streak_aversion_lapse`. Each person has their own ideal switching rate, their own weight on lopsided heads/tails counts and their own weight on the longest streak.\n- **Lapses:** each person still guesses on some trials at their own rate.\n- **The new part:** each person also leans toward one response button. That lean is added to every comparison, so it matters most when the two sequences look about equally random. It is drawn from a population whose average can lean left.\n\n**Why it should count as different.** People chose Left on 51.0% of the 5,120 trials, and individual people's Left rates vary a bit more than chance alone would give (spread 0.073 against an expected 0.062). A model that captures that lean should shift its predictions on new pairs by roughly 0.01, well above the 0.002 threshold. The overall lean is weak, though (about 1.3 standard errors from 50%). If the fitted lean ends up near zero, the shift would be smaller and admission could reject this as a near-duplicate again. Since a second rejection is final, that is the main risk.\n\n**Self-check result:** `OK`, with an ELPD-LOO of −2386.9 on the short fit. Two warnings came with it:\n- R-hat couldn't be computed because the check runs only one chain, so convergence wasn't verified.\n- The ELPD estimate was flagged as unreliable: 5.1% of trials had a high Pareto k, against a 1% tolerance. That comes from a 100-draw single-chain fit and may not hold for admission's full fit, but I haven't confirmed that.\n\nThe palindrome and opening-streak problems in `critiques.md` aren't what this model targets; the brief asked for a decision-rule mechanism rather than new evidence.\n\nAll three files are in `experiment2/model_loop/iter_1/candidate_1_repair_1/`."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3755,"cache_read_input_tokens":69312,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3755,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"aaf6fcc4-c9d3-43f3-bfa2-34a4df230f6a","uuid":"efaa9213-fa36-46f1-95df-b8359722152a","timestamp":"2026-10-01T09:28:27.436Z","request_id":"req_011CfbHDFP8zUnw3Up8pyC6d"}
